        DSS must be 0-3. Optional
␀DDR5 page close timer
Valid values:  0 to 255
Default value is 0
Parameters:
    DSS#: Parameter can be different per DRAM Subsystem (DSS).
        DSS must be 0-3. Optional
␀ High VDD
 When VDD=0.9V nominal, set this to 1'b1, otherwise set to 1'b0
 This input is used to program TxSlewRate[8] and ATxSlewRate[9]
 Values: 0, 1
 Default: 0
␀Pull-up slew rate control for DBYTE transmitter
Value specified here will be applied directly to TxSlewRate::CsrTxSrc
Please consult the Calibration Codes section of HSpice Model App Note in specific technology for recommended settings
 2'b00 = slew rate control off
 2'b01 = slew rate control on, slower edge
 2'b10 = slew rate control on, slow(est) edge
 2'b11 = reserved for future use
␀Pull-down slew rate control for DBYTE transmitter
Value specified here will be applied directly to TxSlewRate::TxPreN
Please consult the Calibration Codes section of HSpice Model App Note in specific technology for recommended settings
 2'b00 = slew rate control off
 2'b01 = slew rate control on, slower edge
 2'b10 = slew rate control on, slow(est) edge
 2'b11 = reserved for future use
␀Pull-up slew rate control for ANIB(ACX4) transmitter
Value specified here will be applied directly to ATxSlewRate::CsrATxSrc
Please consult the Calibration Codes section of HSpice Model App Note in specific technology for recommended settings
WARNING: Vaule of this parameter is don't care if Pull-up slew rate control for ANIB(ACX4) transmitter is set to 0. Refer to ATxSlewRate descriptions in Register Descriptions of PHY Utility Block (PUB) Databook for more informationn
 2'b00 = slew rate control off
 2'b01 = slew rate control on, slower edge
 2'b10 = slew rate control on, slow(est) edge
 2'b11 = reserved for future use
␀Pull-down slew rate control for ANIB(ACX4) transmitter
Value specified here will be applied directly to ATxSlewRate::ATxPreN
Please consult the Calibration Codes section of HSpice Model App Note in specific technology for recommended settings
WARNING: Vaule of this parameter is don't care if Pull-up slew rate control for ANIB(ACX4) transmitter is set to 0. Refer to ATxSlewRate descriptions in Register Descriptions of PHY Utility Block (PUB) Databook for more informationn
 2'b00 = slew rate control off
 2'b01 = slew rate control on, slower edge
 2'b10 = slew rate control on, slow(est) edge
 2'b11 = reserved for future use
␀Pull-up slew rate control for CK
Value specified here will be applied directly to ATxSlewRate::CsrATxSrc
Please consult the Calibration Codes section of HSpice Model App Note in specific technology for recommended settings
WARNING: Vaule of this parameter is don't care if Pull-up slew rate control for ANIB(ACX4) transmitter is set to 0. Refer to ATxSlewRate descriptions in Register Descriptions of PHY Utility Block (PUB) Databook for more informationn
 2'b00 = slew rate control off
 2'b01 = slew rate control on, slower edge
 2'b10 = slew rate control on, slow(est) edge
 2'b11 = reserved for future use
␀Pull-down slew rate control for CK
Value specified here will be applied directly to ATxSlewRate::ATxPreN
Please consult the Calibration Codes section of HSpice Model App Note in specific technology for recommended settings
WARNING: Vaule of this parameter is don't care if Pull-up slew rate control for ANIB(ACX4) transmitter is set to 0. Refer to ATxSlewRate descriptions in Register Descriptions of PHY Utility Block (PUB) Databook for more informationn
 2'b00 = slew rate control off
 2'b01 = slew rate control on, slower edge
 2'b10 = slew rate control on, slow(est) edge
 2'b11 = reserved for future use
␀2N mode CA setup/hold time setting w.r.t. CK capturing CS assertion (default 0)
Value specified here will be applied directly to PHY CSR PhyCfg[2:0]
 2'b00 = 2UI setup time, 2UI hold time (CA stable 1UI before CS falling edge until 1UI after CS rising edge)
 2'b01 = 3UI setup time, 1UI hold time (similar to RCD SDR1 mode)
 2'b10 = 1UI setup time, 3UI hold time (similar to RCD SDR2 mode)
 2'b11 = reserved
␀Some Advanced Training Options (default 0x28, binary'0 01 01 00 0')
 AdvTrainOpt[0]:   Use same data sequence on all DQ during read training (MRR31 based) (1=same,0=diff)
 AdvTrainOpt[2:1]: DQ odd seed shift setting during read training (MRR31 based) (0=1-bit, 1=2-bit)
 AdvTrainOpt[4:3]: Extended sequence for read training (w.r.t. AdvTrainOpt[7] setting)(0=1X, 1=2X, 2=4X, 3=8X)
 AdvTrainOpt[6:5]: Extended sequence for write training (w.r.t. AdvTrainOpt[7] setting)(0=1X, 1=2X, 2=4X, 3=8X)
 AdvTrainOpt[7]:   Use RD/WR burst length twice shorter (0=2048, 1=1024)
␀CA training option bit field (default value 0x10):
    [7]: Use multiple patterns during CA training
    [6]: RFU, must be zero
    [5]: Enable 2D CS training
    [4]: 1 -> Skip CA13 during CA training, 0 -> DO NOT skip
  [3:2]: step size for CS and CA training: 0 -> 1, 1 -> 2, 2 -> 4, 3 -> 8
    [1]: Enable RDIMM/LRDIMM CA DFE training (UDIMM: RFU, must be zero)
    [0]: Enable 2D CA training
Parameters:
    DSS#: Parameter can be different per DRAM Subsystem (DSS).
        DSS must be 0-2. Optional
␀Repeat (or don't repeat) RX training
Valid values:
    0: Don't repeat - Read dqs training is executed only once,
                      by issuing MRR MR31 commands to the DRAM.

    1: Repeat - Read dqs training is executed a second time,
                after write dq training, this time by issuing
                write/read commands to the DRAM with PRBS23 patterns.
Parameters:
    DSS#: Parameter can be different per DRAM Subsystem (DSS).
        DSS must be 0-2. Optional
␀Use DRAM LFSR0 only or use both LFSR
Valid values:
    0: Both LFSR are used to create, together with inversion, different
       patterns on each DQ of a nibble during MRR MR31

    1: Only LFSR0 is used: this is required for x16 devices in case
       swizzle configuration is not the same between lower and upper byte
Parameters:
    DSS#: Parameter can be different per DRAM Subsystem (DSS).
        DSS must be 0-2. Optional
␀Low bound of the Vref sweep range used during VrefCS training
Note: Min corresponds to the lowest voltage setting
(i.e. the highest valid VrefCS code).
Valid values: 0 to 255
Parameters:
    DSS#: Parameter can be different per DRAM Subsystem (DSS).
        DSS must be 0-2. Optional
␀High bound of the Vref sweep range used during VrefCS training
Note: the sweep range is [VrefCS_Sweep_Min, VrefCS_Sweep_Max[
(i.e. the max value is not included in the sweep). If left at 0,
the max Vref will be used as high bound.
Valid values: 0 to 255
Parameters:
    DSS#: Parameter can be different per DRAM Subsystem (DSS).
        DSS must be 0-2. Optional
␀Low bound of the Vref sweep range used during VrefCA training
Note: Min corresponds to the lowest voltage setting
(i.e. the highest valid VrefCA code).
Valid values: 0 to 255
Parameters:
    DSS#: Parameter can be different per DRAM Subsystem (DSS).
        DSS must be 0-2. Optional
␀High bound of the Vref sweep range used during VrefCA training
Note: the sweep range is [VrefCA_Sweep_Min, VrefCA_Sweep_Max[
(i.e. the max value is not included in the sweep). If left at 0,
the max Vref will be used as high bound.
Valid values: 0 to 255
Parameters:
    DSS#: Parameter can be different per DRAM Subsystem (DSS).
        DSS must be 0-2. Optional
␀DDR Memory Built-In Self Test (mBIST) to run at boot
Control DRAM mBIST to help with hard failures such as single-bit or multi-bit failures
    0: Disable mBIST for current DSS
    [1 - 10]: Enable and execute mBIST specified number of times for current DSS
              Specify maximum number of mBIST to run if MR22 specify mBIST should be run again
              Do not execute mPPR (should be enabled separately)
mBIST menu is optional if mPPR enabled this mBIST execution counter set to 1 automatically
␀mBIST-mPPR Post Package Repair (mPPR) and Memory Built-In Self Test to run at boot
Allows optional mPPR to help with hard failures such as single-bit or multi-bit failures
    0: Disable mPPR for current DSS
    1: Enable mPPR for current DSS
       Enabled mPPR automatically enable mBIST with execution counter set to 1
␀mBIST Read Transparency Register to determine next steps
If unrepairable fails remain:
    0: Proceed to boot
    1: Halt and report error
␀DDR ECC Scrubber period is 24 hours allow disable/enable and select mode
Valid values:
    0:  Disabled (default)
    1:  Background Scrubber Enable
    2:  On-Demand  Scrubber Enable
    3:  Background and On-Demand  Scrubber Enable
    4:  Background RMW and On-Demand Scrubber Enable (MCT supports this mode only)
Parameters:
    DSS#: Parameter can be different per DRAM Subsystem (DSS).
        DSS must be 0-3. Optional
␀Diagnostic test to validate the operation of preserving memory across WARM
system resets. When enabled, will infinite-loop doing WARM resets and testing
the preserved area contents for corruption.
␀Normal boot test to check on the operation of preserving memory across WARM
system resets. When enabled, it will perform a read-only test of the preserved
area after a WARM reset, and issue a COLD reset if any double-bit ECC
errors are found.
␀Allows optional specification of memory testing during normal boots.
    0 = NONE     - no testing
    1 = NOMINAL  - quick test
    2 = EXTENDED - slow test
␀Starting physical address for automated memory tests.
Input of 0 will default to 16MB.
␀Length of area used for automated memory tests.
Input of -1 will default to all of memory between
the starting address and the start of preserved areas.
Input of 0 will default to all memory locations between
the starting address and the end of physical memory.
␀Flags that control output during DRAM test execution; bit significant.
  NO_STOP_ERROR = 1; do not stop running the test when an error occurs.
  NO_PROGRESS   = 2; do not print progress indication during the test.
  NO_STATS      = 4; do not print performance statics at the end of the test.
  NO_BANNERS    = 8; do not print a banners during a test run.
Flags can be combined; default is NO_STATS.
␀Specify a on-chip GPIO used to control power for a USB port. When
set, the USB host controller will toggle the GPIO automatically
during USB reset events. The default value assumes the SOC has no
control of the USB power and it is always on.␀Specify the polarity of a on-chip GPIO used to control power for
a USB port. The GPIO used is specified by USB-PWR-GPIO. Setting this
GPIO low. The default value drives the GPIO high.␀Bitmap of USB ports to disable
␀Value for USBHX_UAHC_LCSR_TX_DEEMPH for 5GHz for A0 silicon.
␀Value for USBHX_UAHC_LCSR_TX_DEEMPH for 10GHz for A0 silicon.
␀Value for USBHX_UAHC_LCSR_TX_DEEMPH_1 for A0 silicon
␀Value for USBHX_UAHC_LCSR_TX_DEEMPH_2 for A0 silicon
␀Value for USBHX_UAHC_LCSR_TX_DEEMPH_3 for A0 silicon
␀Optimize USB SS for either 5G or 10G for A0 silicon
Field values: 0 - auto, no override
              1 - 5G
              2 - 10G
␀Enable USB signal detect LF threshold override value
␀Temperature where THERMAL_TRIP_N is asserted. When the chip reaches
this temperature THERMAL_TRIP_N will assert, signalling the board
to emergency power off. The default value is the recommended
maximum temperature of the chip. Values between 0 and 140 degrees
Celsius.
␀throttle_temp:  in all modes, this defines the temperature at which
the reported thermal_margin = 0.  When DVFS_POWER_CONTROL_MODE = THERMAL_BUDGET
or POWER_BUDGET, it also is the temperature at which coreclks begin to be 
throttled to keep the die  at or below this temperature.
The default value is the
recommended maximum operating temperature of the chip. Values
between 0 and 140 degrees Celsius.
␀Maximum coreclk[i] frequency in MHz.  When DVFS_POWER_CONTROL_MODE =
DVFS, it is the default frequency of all cores, and the highest
frequency advertised in scaling_available_frequencies.  When
DVFS_POWER_CONTROL_MODE = POWER_BUDGET or THERMAL_BUDGET, it is
the default frequency of all cores, but coreclk[i] may be reduced
if needed to stay under the thermal and power budgets.  If fuses
indicate the part supports a lower frequency, the fuse value is
used.  Valid range is 500MHz to SKU max frequency.
␀Minimum default coreclk[i] frequency in MHz.
When DVFS_POWER_CONTROL_MODE = THERMAL_BUDGET or POWER_BUDGET,
coreclk[i] are set to this value when all cores are idle.
Unused when DVFS_POWER_CONTROL_MODE = DVFS
Valid range is 500MHz to SKU max frequency.
␀Maximum Coprocessor clock (SCLK) frequency in MHz. Valid range is
350MHz-1200MHz, SKU dependant.
␀Minimum Coprocessor clock (SCLK) frequency in MHz. Valid range is
350MHz-1200MHz, SKU dependant.
␀bphy clock (BCLK) frequency in MHz. SCP firmware can update
BCLK frequency based on SCMI messages from the AP.
␀dsp clock (DSPCLK) frequency in MHz. SCP firmware can update
DSPCLK frequency based on SCMI messages from the AP.
␀cpt clock (CPTCLK) frequency in MHz (only to cn10kb).
␀IO clock frequency in MHz.  Valid range is 400MHz-800MHz.
␀Ethernet clock (NETCLK) frequency in MHz. Valid range is 300MHz-800MHz
␀NCLK clock frequency in MHz.  Valid range is 400MHz-1000MHz.
␀Specify whether GSERM will use synchronous Ethernet Mode
or non-synchronous Ethernet Mode
Possible SYNCE-MODE options:
    1 = Use synchronous Ethernet Mode (default)
    0 = Use non-synchronous Ethernet Mode
NOTE: This setting applies to cnf10ka and cnf10kb
The format of the key is:
SYNCE-MODE.GSER#
Parameters:
    GSER#: Parameter can be different for each GSERM.
␀The +/- control tolerance of the vdd_core supply as measured at
the Chip in mV. Default is +/-29mV.
␀The compensation factor in percentage for the vdd_core supply.
This can be used to adjust the voltage programmed by firmware to
account for customer-specific VRM design requirements.  An example
usage is below.
    VDDC required voltage = 800mV
    Compensation percentage = 95%
    VDDC actual programmed voltage = 760mV
This should be set to 100% for most designs.  Default is 100.
␀The +/- control tolerance of the vdd_sys supply as measured at
the Chip in mV. Default is +/-29mV.
␀The compensation factor in percentage for the vdd_sys supply.
This can be used to adjust the voltage programmed by firmware to
account for customer-specific VRM design requirements.  An example
usage is below.
    VDD_SYS required voltage = 800mV
    Compensation percentage = 95%
    VDD_SYS actual programmed voltage = 760mV
This should be set to 100% for most designs.  Default is 100.
␀The total chip power budget in Watts, used when
DVFS_POWER_CONTROL_MODE = POWER_BUDGET.  In this mode, the
coreclk[i] are throttled as needed to keep power at or below
DVFS_POWER_BUDGET and die temperature below VRM_TEMP_HIGH.
Values between 0 and
150 Watts.  Default is 100W.
␀The operating mode of the DVFS (Dynamic Frequency Voltage Scaling)
power control code.  Encoding:
    1 - POWER_BUDGET: stay below power and thermal budgets
    2 - THERMAL_BUDGET: Stay below thermal budget
    3-4 - Reserved.
    5 - DVFS: Operate only at CORECLK_FREQ_MAX (NO throttling).
Default is 5 (DVFS)
␀Chip will assert the THERMAL_HOT_L pin when the highest
observed temperature on the chip is at or above this value.
Intended for simple two state fan control.
Values between 0 and 140 degrees Celsius.
␀The percentage that the DVFS control should attempt to reduce the chip
power when the board asserts THERMAL_HOT.  A value of 30 means reduce
power 30%, etc.  Allowed values 0 to 100.  Default is 50.
␀This timeout detects boot failures in when booting from the primary
boot device, and causes a reboot from secondary. In seconds.
␀Selects which uart SCP will use for the SCP CLI and logging.
0xf selects no logging.
␀Selects a uart which is internally redirected (connected) to the SCP CLI UART.
0xf selects no redirection..
␀Instructs SCP firmware to not start the MCP.
␀Force the next reboot to use the secondary boot device.
␀Enumerated by CPC_WAKEUP_MODE_E. Default FAST_WAKE_SLOW_SLEEP.
    0 = FAST_WAKE_OFF: When AP activity detected, ramp from min_rclk
        to max_rclk over ~10ms.
    1 = FAST_WAKE_SLOW_SLEEP: When AP activity detected, ramp from
        min_rclk to max_rclk in 1ms. When activity ceases, ramp from
        max_rclk to min_rclk over ~10ms.
    2 = FAST_WAKE_FAST_SLEEP: When AP activity detected, ramp from
        min_rclk to max_rclk in 1ms. When activity ceases, ramp from
        max_rclk to min_rclk IN 1ms.
␀The percentage of maximum IPC at which a core is considered active.
Encoded as percent * 1000. Range 0.001% to 32.7%. Default 1%.
␀Timeout in seconds for SCP to configure clocks and signal complete to
the EBF.
␀Action to take if the SCP fail timeout expires:
    0: Continue to boot (Default)
    1: Reboot the system
    2: Halt forever
␀Action to take if the SCP loses AVSBus communication with the voltage regulator:
   0: No changes are made to clock frequencies.
   1: If 10 attempts by SCP to set the VDDC or VDD_SYS voltage fail,
       CORECLK, MESHCLK and CPT_CLK are lowered to a safe frequency.
␀The number of twsi controller that processes twsi target transactions
from chip side for external power and temperature access. Set to -1
to disable.  Default is -1.
␀If using the TWSI target bus feature, this is the TWSI address of
the target controller.  Default is 0x77.
␀SCP asserts this GPIO when it takes an NMI.
␀Configure the Kernel to MCP Channel address. The encoding of this
value is the exact address that mapping to Linux dts. Default is 0.
␀Configure the Kernel to MCP Channel length. The encoding of this
value is the exact length that mapping to Linux dts. Default is 0.
␀Configure the MCP to Kernel Circular Buffer Channel address. The
encoding of this value is the exact address that mapping to Linux dts.
Default is 0.
␀Configure the MCP to Kernel Circular Buffer Channel length. The
encoding of this value is the exact length that mapping to Linux dts.
Default is 0.
␀Disable TAD early DQ (Applies only to cn10ka A0 and cnf10ka A0 pass).
␀Internal source for GPIO pins. The enumeration GPIO_PIN_SEL_E
details the possible internal sources for GPIO pins in the chip.
The value of this setting is the numeric value from the GPIO_PIN_SEL_E
enumeration. The default value(-1) leaves the pin at the hardware
default, which is to connect the pin to the GPIO block for manual
control.
Parameters:
    GPIO#: Which GPIO pin to configure.
␀Set the polarity of GPIO pins. GPIO pins are normally high=1 and
low=0. Setting this configuration item to '1' inverts this, so
high=0 and low=1. The inversion applies to both input and output.
The default value of '0' configures high=1 and low=0.
Parameters:
    GPIO#: Which GPIO pin to configure.
␀GPIO pin output slew rate control.
    0 = Weakest slew rate.
    3 = Strongest slew rate. (Default)
Parameters:
    GROUP#: GROUP0 = GPIO 0-15, GROUP1 = GPIO 31-16, GROUP2 = GPIO 32-47, GROUP3 = GPIO 48-63 
    And GROUP4 = GPIO 64-79, GROUP5 = GPIO 80-95, GROUP6 = GPIO 96-110, GROUP7 = GPIO 112-127 are optional 
␀GPIO pin output drive strength.
    0x0 = 55 ohm
    0x1 = 45 ohm
    0x2 = 33 ohm (Default)
    0x3 = 20 ohm
Parameters:
    GROUP#: GROUP0 = GPIO 0-15, GROUP1 = GPIO 31-16, GROUP2 = GPIO 32-47, GROUP3 = GPIO 48-63 
    And GROUP4 = GPIO 64-79, GROUP5 = GPIO 80-95, GROUP6 = GPIO 96-110, GROUP7 = GPIO 112-127 are optional 
␀Same as EBF_CONFIG_GPIO_PIN_SELECT, but executed at init_complete stage
Parameters:
    GPIO#: Which GPIO pin to configure.
␀Same as EBF_CONFIG_GPIO_POLARITY, but executed at init_complete stage
Parameters:
    GPIO#: Which GPIO pin to configure.
␀AVS pin output slew rate control.
    0 = Weakest slew rate.
    3 = Strongest slew rate. (Default)␀AVS pin output drive strength.
    0x0 = 55 ohms
    0x1 = 45 ohms
    0x2 = 33 ohms
    0x3 = 20 ohms (Default)
␀TWSI bus pin output slew rate control.
    0 = Freq < 50mhz
    1 = Freq 50mhz - 100mhz
    2 = Freq 100mhz - 150mhz (Default)
    3 = Freq > 150mhz
Parameters:
    TWSI#: TWSI block to program.␀TWSI bus pin output impedance.
    0x0 = 55 ohms
    0x1 = 45 ohms (Default)
    0x2 = 33 ohms
    0x3 = 20 ohms
Parameters:
    TWSI#: TWSI block to program.␀TWSI bus pin output slew rate control.
    0 = Weakest
    3 = Strongest (Default)
Parameters:
    UAA#: UAA[0..1] block to program. For other UARTS use GPIO_SLEW.␀TWSI bus pin output impedance.
    0x0 = 55 ohms
    0x1 = 45 ohms
    0x2 = 33 ohms
    0x3 = 20 ohms (Default)
Parameters:
    UAA#: UAA[0..1] block to program. For other UARTS use GPIO_DRIVE.␀Configure a PBUS region. The encoding of this value is the exact
register contents of PBUS_REGX_CFG. Note that the base is
overwritten by the EBF to fix addressing in the code.
Parameters:
    REGION#: Which PBUS region to configure.
␀Configure a PBUS region timing. The encoding of this value is the
exact register contents of PBUS_REGX_TIM.
Parameters:
    REGION#: Which PBUS region to configure.
␀To count and save reset counters in persist region or not.
By default counters will be not saved to persist region
␀Warm Reset Counter information, is saved in SPI flash and updated on every warm reset.Software sets count at run time
␀Cold Reset Counter information, is saved in SPI flash and updated on every cold reset.Software sets count at run time
␀Count SCP Watchdog information, is saved in SPI flash and updated on every watchdog for SCP.Software sets count at run time
␀Count Core Watchdog information, is saved in SPI flash and updated on every watchdog from Core.Software sets count at run time
␀Count MCP Watchdog information, is saved in SPI flash and updated on every watchdog for MCP.Software sets count at run time
␀Count ECP Watchdog information, is saved in SPI flash and updated on every watchdog for ECP.Software sets count at run time
␀SPI NOR Flash supports 64K sectors only, otherwise usually 4K.
Value of 1 forces 64K erase size.
Parameters:
    Bus#: Must be 0 or 1.
    CS#: Parameter for selecting chip-select for SPI Bus.
␀Load auxiliary firmware to the EHSM via the PIE loading method.
␀(JESD204C_24_3G specific) Configure Phase Adaptation Calibration Value
used for further tuning. The rx_align90_cal_setting_lane[7:0] is gradually
changed to this desired setting, one code at a time.Possible PHASE_ADAPT_CAL range from 0 to 64. Default is 15 (0xf)
NOTE: This setting only applies to cnf10ka.
The format of the key is:
   PHASE-ADAPT-CAL.GSER#
Parameters:
   GSER#: Parameter can be different for each GSER. Optional
␀Delay PCIe endpoint initialization until memory configuration has completed.
This is required in order to use the host [PCIe] remote console while rebooting
the Octeon processor.  NOTE: this may slightly increase boot time.
This value is only applicable to Endpoint mode.
0 means to initialize PCIe as soon as possible.1 means to wait for memory configuration to complete before initializing PCIe.
Default value is 1.
␀Disable UART0 Rx upon boot to disable serial console input (for security purposes).
This is a list of strings, each containing a specific <board_revision> for which
the console disablement is desired.  This allows control based on board revision.
To enable for ANY <board_revision>, specify "*".
To disable this feature and allow normal console operation,
specify an empty string ("").
The default value is an empty string (""), which disables this feature.
␀Use custom Identity for PCIe Endpoint to change identity seen by the host.
The Identity values which must be configured are:
    PCI Device Id  (represented in DTS via EBF_CONFIG_PCIE_EP_ID)
    PCI Class Code (represented in DTS via EBF_CONFIG_PCIE_EP_REV)
This value is only applicable to Endpoint mode.
Note: this item is set automatically if the PCIe Identify is configured via the setup menu.
0 means to use pre-configured PCI Endpoint Identify values.
1 means to use PCI Endpoint Identify values from DTS.
-1 is used internally to indicate that this item is uninitialized.
Default value is -1.
␀Size of non-secure user defined DRAM region (in units of MB) for coresight to
preserve across system resets. Must be a multiple of 16MB. Min is 0. Max is 64MB.
␀This specifies a APA watchdog transaction timeout for core to receive response.
The timeout is specified in milliseconds, typically less than 10ms. Zero disables
the APA watchdog.
␀Increase security by disabling host access to Octeon windowing registers via BAR0.
NOTE: this disables certain host activities, such as firmware update.
Default value is 0, permitting host access; a value of 1 disables host access.
␀Hide TWSI bus from Non-secure world.
